\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study estimation of the asymptotic floor $L$ of a fading measurement ladder $\rho_0,\rho_1,\dots$ whose residuals $s_k=\rho_k-L$ are bounded, $|s_k|\le\eta$, and whose sign pattern decomposes into $m$ maximal alternating constant-sign blocks of lengths $n_1,\dots,n_m$. The natural estimator giving each block equal total weight --- weight $1/n_i$ per rung of block $i$, so that the estimate is the arithmetic mean of the $m$ block means --- was conjectured to achieve worst-case error $2\eta/m$. We prove the sharp law and refute the conjecture. The block-balanced estimator satisfies $|\widehat L_{\mathrm{bb}}-L|\le \eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$, a bound in which the block lengths do not appear, and this is an equality for an explicit admissible ladder. Hence the worst-case error is at least $\eta/2$ for every $m$, and from $m\ge 5$ it strictly exceeds $2\eta/m$. The failure is structural: for \emph{any} nonnegative block weights summing to one, one of two explicit extremal ladders is estimated with error at least $\eta/2$; and a single ladder of readings is realised exactly by the floor $L$ with saturating positive blocks and by $L+\eta$ with saturating negative blocks, so every procedure whatsoever errs by $\eta/2$ on one of the two worlds. The nonlinear midrange attains $\eta/2$, so the minimax floor error is exactly $\eta/2$. Under \emph{exact} amplitude the same estimator does achieve $\eta/m$ for arbitrary block lengths. Applied to the motivating record, whose first positive step measured $+0.0226$ and thereby forces $\eta\ge 0.0226$, no number of blocks brings the worst-case floor estimate below $\pm 0.0113$ --- reproducing, by a purely combinatorial route, a resolution floor previously derived from the analytic fade model.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical setting}

A dial experiment records a pooled rank correlation $\rho$ between two quantities as a discrete control parameter is increased. Successive rungs read
\[
0.5739,\quad 0.5436,\quad 0.5005,\quad 0.4880,\quad 0.4621,\quad 0.4847,
\]
five decreases followed by a single increase of $+0.0226$. Two generative stories competed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a \emph{pure multiplicative fade}, $\rho_{k+1}\le q\rho_k$ with $q<1$ and $\rho_k\ge 0$ --- a slide to zero;
  \item an \emph{affine fade toward a positive floor with rebound noise}, $\rho_{k+1}-L=\lambda(\rho_k-L)+\varepsilon_k$ with $|\varepsilon_k|\le\eta$, $|\lambda|<1$.
\end{itemize}
The first is refuted by a single positive step, with no statistics: if $q\le 1$ and $\rho_k\ge 0$ then $\rho_{k+1}\le q\rho_k\le\rho_k$, so no step can be positive. In the second, the same step is informative: in a non-expanding model ($\lambda\le 1$) sitting above its floor, a positive step of size $\delta$ forces $\eta\ge\delta$. The observed rebound therefore certifies $\eta\ge 0.0226$. Accepting the floor story, the operative question is: how accurately can $L$ be estimated?

\subsection{Prior layers}

Three structural facts frame the work.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Identifiability threshold.} If one ladder is a noisy affine fade with ratio $\lambda$ and noise $\eta$ for \emph{both} floors $L_1,L_2$, then $|1-\lambda|\,|L_1-L_2|\le 2\eta$; conversely, whenever that holds, an explicit constant ladder at the midpoint realises both models exactly. Two floors are thus confusable at noise $\eta$ precisely when $|1-\lambda|\,|L_1-L_2|\le 2\eta$, so floor resolution is $2\eta/|1-\lambda|$.
  \item \textbf{Sign changes cap drift, under exact amplitude.} For a $\pm1$ sequence $e_0,\dots,e_K$ with $c$ adjacent sign changes, $|\sum_{k\le K}e_k|\le (K+1)-c$, sharp at both extremes; hence the mean of $K+1$ residuals of amplitude exactly $\eta$ with $c$ sign changes is at most $\eta((K+1)-c)/(K+1)$.
  \item \textbf{Majority-count drift law, under boundedness.} That conversion collapses when amplitudes are merely bounded: with $A$ the number of rungs carrying the sign of the last one and $B$ the number carrying the opposite sign, $|\sum_k s_k|\le\eta\max(A,B)$, and this is attained. Four residuals bounded by $1$ with three sign changes can drift by $3/2$ against the $1$ predicted by the exact-amplitude law; half of each sign change survives, $|\sum_k s_k|\le\eta((K+1)-c/2)$, the constant $1/2$ being optimal.
\end{enumerate}
Item 3 says the \emph{unweighted} mean of $K+1$ rungs has worst-case error $\eta\max(A,B)/(K+1)$, which does not decay for unbalanced patterns. Hence the estimator studied here.

\subsection{The conjecture and its fate}

Give each rung of a block of length $n_i$ the weight $1/n_i$, so each of the $m$ blocks carries total weight $1$ and no long block shouts down a short one. The conjectured worst-case error was $2\eta/m$. It is false, and its failure is total: the exact worst case is $\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m\ge\eta/2$, no reweighting and indeed no procedure whatsoever does better than $\eta/2$, and the simplest nonlinear procedure attains it.

\section{Setup and definitions}

Throughout $\eta\ge 0$ and $m\ge 1$.

\begin{definition}[Block data]
A \emph{block configuration} is a sequence of block lengths $n_i\ge 1$ together with a residual array $s_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}$, where $i$ indexes the block and $0\le j<n_i$ the rung inside it. The readings are $\rho_{i,j}=L+s_{i,j}$ for an unknown floor $L$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissibility]
Fix $\epsilon\in\{+1,-1\}$. The array $s$ is \emph{$(\eta,\epsilon)$-admissible} if
\begin{enumerate}
  \item (boundedness) $|s_{i,j}|\le\eta$ for all $i,j$; and
  \item (weak alternation) $\epsilon(-1)^i s_{i,j}\ge 0$ for all $i,j$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Weak alternation says the residuals of block $i$ lie on the side of zero dictated by the parity of $i$, with $0$ lying on both sides. Strictness cannot be demanded --- a residual may legitimately vanish --- and it is exactly this slack that drives everything below.

\begin{definition}[Block sums, block means, estimators]
Put $S_i=\sum_{j<n_i}s_{i,j}$, with block mean $S_i/n_i$. The \emph{block-balanced} floor estimate is
\[
\widehat L_{\mathrm{bb}}=\frac1m\sum_{i<m}\frac1{n_i}\sum_{j<n_i}\rho_{i,j},
\qquad\text{so}\qquad
\widehat L_{\mathrm{bb}}-L=\BB(s):=\frac1m\sum_{i<m}\frac{S_i}{n_i}.
\]
For weights $w_0,\dots,w_{m-1}$ put $\Wt_w(s)=\sum_{i<m}w_i S_i/n_i$; thus $\BB=\Wt_w$ with $w_i\equiv 1/m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Extremal ladders]\label{def:extremal}
For $\eta\ge 0$, with all blocks singletons ($n_i=1$),
\[
P_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\eta,& i\text{ even}\\ 0,& i\text{ odd}\end{cases}
\qquad
N_{i,j}=\begin{cases}0,& i\text{ even}\\ -\eta,& i\text{ odd.}\end{cases}
\]
Both are $(\eta,+1)$-admissible. $P$ saturates the positive blocks and silences the negative ones; $N$ is its mirror image.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Midrange]
For readings $x$ and window size $m+1$, $\MR(x,m)=\tfrac12(\max_{k\le m}x_k+\min_{k\le m}x_k)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Alternating sequences of bounded terms barely cancel}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided invariant]\label{thm:invariant}
Let $|a_i|\le\eta$ and $(-1)^i a_i\ge 0$ for all $i$. Then for every $m\ge 0$,
\[
-\eta\Bigl\lfloor \frac m2\Bigr\rfloor \;\le\; \sum_{i<m}a_i \;\le\; \eta\Bigl\lceil \frac m2\Bigr\rceil .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
First $\eta\ge|a_0|\ge 0$. Induct on $m$ carrying \emph{both} inequalities. If $m$ is even then $(-1)^m=1$, so $0\le a_m\le\eta$: the upper budget grows, $\lceil (m+1)/2\rceil=\lceil m/2\rceil+1$, absorbing $+\eta$, while the lower budget is unchanged and the inequality is only helped by $a_m\ge 0$. If $m$ is odd then $-\eta\le a_m\le 0$ and the roles are exchanged. The asymmetry between the two sides is a whole half of the length and never collapses: a term of the ``wrong'' sign for the bound being tested may vanish, so no cancellation is available.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sign-symmetric form]\label{cor:symmetric}
If $|a_i|\le\eta$ and $\epsilon(-1)^i a_i\ge 0$ for all $i$ with $\epsilon\in\{\pm1\}$, then $\bigl|\sum_{i<m}a_i\bigr|\le\eta\lceil m/2\rceil$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Replace $a_i$ by $\epsilon a_i$, which preserves the bound and yields the positive-first alternation of Theorem~\ref{thm:invariant}; symmetrise using $\lfloor m/2\rfloor\le\lceil m/2\rceil$ and undo the multiplication using $|\epsilon|=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
If instead $a_i=(-1)^i\eta$ exactly, then $\sum_{i<m}a_i\in\{0,\eta\}$, bounded uniformly in $m$. Corollary~\ref{cor:symmetric} is worse by the factor $\lceil m/2\rceil$; Section~\ref{sec:salvage} shows the loss is real.
\end{remark}

\section{The block-balanced law, and the refutation}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bdd}
If $n_i\ge 1$ and $|s_{i,j}|\le\eta$ for all $j<n_i$, then $|S_i/n_i|\le\eta$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|S_i|\le\sum_{j<n_i}|s_{i,j}|\le n_i\eta$; divide by $n_i>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sign}
Under weak alternation with starting sign $\epsilon$, $\epsilon(-1)^i(S_i/n_i)\ge 0$ for all $i$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\epsilon(-1)^iS_i=\sum_{j<n_i}\epsilon(-1)^is_{i,j}$ is a sum of nonnegative terms; dividing by $n_i>0$ preserves the sign.
\end{proof}

These lemmas say the block means form exactly the kind of sequence Corollary~\ref{cor:symmetric} governs, and nothing more: passing to block means destroys all information about block lengths.

\begin{theorem}[Block-balanced law]\label{thm:bb}
Let $s$ be $(\eta,\epsilon)$-admissible on a configuration with $n_i\ge 1$, and $m\ge1$. Then
\[
|\BB(s)|\;\le\;\frac{\eta\lceil m/2\rceil}{m}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:symmetric} to $a_i=S_i/n_i$, legitimated by Lemmas~\ref{lem:bdd} and \ref{lem:sign}, and divide by $m>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The block lengths have disappeared: only their number matters. Block balancing \emph{does} remove the imbalance $\max(A,B)$ of the majority-count law --- while failing to deliver consistency.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
With singleton blocks and the extremal ladder $P$ of Definition~\ref{def:extremal}, $\BB(P)=\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each block mean equals $\eta$ if $i$ is even and $0$ otherwise; an induction on $m$ splitting on parity evaluates $\sum_{i<m}\eta[i\text{ even}]=\eta\lceil m/2\rceil$, since exactly $\lceil m/2\rceil$ of $0,\dots,m-1$ are even. Divide by $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The conjectured rate is false]
For $\eta>0$ and every $m\ge 5$, $\;2\eta/m<\BB(P)=\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $m\ge5$, $\lceil m/2\rceil\ge 3>2$; multiply by $\eta/m>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No decay]\label{thm:nodecay}
For $\eta\ge0$ and every $m\ge1$, $\;\eta/2\le\BB(P)=\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$m\le 2\lceil m/2\rceil$; divide by $2m$ and multiply by $\eta$.
\end{proof}

