\section{The salvage: exact amplitude restores the decay}\label{sec:salvage}

\begin{theorem}[Exact-amplitude decay]\label{thm:exact}
Fix any block configuration with $n_i\ge1$, let $\eta\ge0$, $m\ge1$, and take the saturated array $s_{i,j}=(-1)^i\eta$. Then $|\BB(s)|\le\eta/m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each block mean is exactly $(-1)^i\eta$, independently of $n_i$. Hence $\BB(s)=\frac\eta m\sum_{i<m}(-1)^i$, and $\sum_{i<m}(-1)^i$ is $0$ for even $m$ and $1$ for odd $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy at a fixed configuration]
For $\eta\ge0$, $m\ge1$ and singleton blocks, simultaneously
\[
\BB(P)\ \ge\ \frac\eta2
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\bigl|\BB\bigl((-1)^i\eta\bigr)\bigr|\ \le\ \frac\eta m .
\]
\end{theorem}

Same number of blocks, same lengths, same amplitude bound, same estimator --- errors differing by a factor $m/2$ that grows without bound. The relevant question about rebound noise is not ``how large is it?'' but ``is it saturated?''

\section{The failure is not about the weights}

\begin{theorem}[No weighting beats half]\label{thm:noweight}
Let $\eta\ge0$, $m\ge1$, and $w_0,\dots,w_{m-1}\ge0$ with $\sum_{i<m}w_i=1$. Then, with $P,N$ as in Definition~\ref{def:extremal},
\[
\frac\eta2\ \le\ \max\bigl(|\Wt_w(P)|,\ |\Wt_w(N)|\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Wt_w(P)=\Pi:=\sum_{i<m}w_i\eta[i\text{ even}]$ and $\Wt_w(N)=-\mathrm{N}$ with $\mathrm{N}:=\sum_{i<m}w_i\eta[i\text{ odd}]$; both are nonnegative since the weights are. Pointwise $w_i\eta[i\text{ even}]+w_i\eta[i\text{ odd}]=w_i\eta$, so $\Pi+\mathrm{N}=\eta\sum_i w_i=\eta$. Two nonnegative reals summing to $\eta$ cannot both be $<\eta/2$.
\end{proof}

The two extremal ladders \emph{partition the total weight}: whatever mass a weighting places on even blocks it loses on $P$, whatever it places on odd blocks it loses on $N$, and the two masses add to one. No allocation escapes. Theorem~\ref{thm:noweight} subsumes Theorem~\ref{thm:nodecay} and rules out length-proportional weighting, exponential discounting, trimming, and every other nonnegative scheme.

\section{The barrier is information-theoretic, and the midrange attains it}

\begin{definition}[Colliding observation]\label{def:collide}
For $L\in\mathbb{R}$, $\eta\ge0$ put $x_k=L+\eta$ for even $k$ and $x_k=L$ for odd $k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Two exact readings of one ladder]
For every $k$, $\;x_k=L+P_{k,0}$ and $x_k=(L+\eta)+N_{k,0}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split on the parity of $k$: for even $k$, $L+\eta=(L+\eta)+0$; for odd $k$, $L=L+0=(L+\eta)-\eta$.
\end{proof}

Thus one observation is an exact realisation of the floor $L$ with $(\eta,+1)$-admissible residuals, and of the floor $L+\eta$ with $(\eta,+1)$-admissible residuals.

\begin{theorem}[Half-amplitude minimax barrier]\label{thm:minimax}
Let $E$ be \emph{any} map from ladders of readings to real numbers. With $x$ the colliding observation of Definition~\ref{def:collide},
\[
\frac\eta2\ \le\ \max\bigl(|E(x)-L|,\ |E(x)-(L+\eta)|\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $y=E(x)$. If $|y-L|\ge\eta/2$ we are done; otherwise $y-L<\eta/2$, so $(L+\eta)-y>\eta/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The midrange attains the barrier]\label{thm:midrange}
Let $|s_k|\le\eta$ and $(-1)^ks_k\ge0$ for all $k$. Then for every window size $m\ge1$,
\[
\bigl|\MR\bigl((L+s_k)_k,m\bigr)-L\bigr|\ \le\ \frac\eta2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $M,\mu$ be the max and min of $L+s_k$ over $0\le k\le m$. Boundedness gives $M\le L+\eta$, $\mu\ge L-\eta$. Alternation gives $s_0\ge0$, $s_1\le 0$, and both indices lie in the window, so $M\ge L$ and $\mu\le L$. Hence $\tfrac12(M+\mu)\in[L-\eta/2,\ L+\eta/2]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact minimax rate]
The minimax worst-case error of floor estimation from a ladder with $(\eta,\epsilon)$-admissible residuals containing at least one rung of each sign is exactly $\eta/2$: no procedure achieves less (Theorem~\ref{thm:minimax}), the midrange achieves it (Theorem~\ref{thm:midrange}), and no nonnegative weighted mean of block means does (Theorem~\ref{thm:noweight}).
\end{corollary}

The optimal procedure discards all but two data points, whereas the estimators that are efficient in a Gaussian world fail here. Under bounded, adversarial, sign-structured noise, extremes carry the information and averages destroy it.

\section{Application to the recorded ladder}

\begin{proposition}
The recorded rebound is $0.4847-0.4621=0.0226$, and in a non-expanding affine floor model above its floor a positive step of size $\delta$ forces $\eta\ge\delta$; take $\eta=0.0226$. Then for every $m\ge1$, $\BB(P)\ge 0.0113$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:nodecay} with $\eta=226/10000$.
\end{proof}

Two independent routes converge. \emph{Analytically}, the identifiability threshold gives resolution $2\eta/|1-\lambda|\ge 0.0226$ for $|\lambda|\le1$, i.e. $\pm0.0113$; this argument knows about $\lambda$. \emph{Combinatorially}, the proposition never mentions $\lambda$ and reaches the same number. A rebound of size $\eta$ simultaneously proves that the noise is at least $\eta$ and caps resolution at $\eta/2$.

The experiment pre-registered the floor window $[0.46,0.49]$ of width $0.03$, while the theory certifies that no honest window is narrower than about $0.0226$. Two structurally different point estimates --- a three-point extrapolation fit giving $\approx 0.474169$ and the plain mean of the last three rungs giving $\approx 0.478267$ --- both land inside the window and agree to within $0.0041$. The pre-registration was slightly conservative, not over-precise.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Block decomposition.} From readings $\rho_0,\dots,\rho_K$ and a floor hypothesis $L$, form $s_k=\rho_k-L$ and split into maximal constant-sign runs (treating $0$ as compatible with either neighbour); output lengths and block sums. Cost $O(K)$.

\paragraph{B. Block-balanced estimate with certified bar.} Return $\widehat L_{\mathrm{bb}}=\frac1m\sum_i\bar\rho_i$ with the two-sided bar $\pm\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$. Cost $O(K)$. The bar is sharp by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.

\paragraph{C. Minimax-optimal midrange.} Return $\tfrac12(\max_k\rho_k+\min_k\rho_k)$ with the bar $\pm\eta/2$, valid whenever the window contains a rung on each side of the floor. Single pass, $O(K)$ time, $O(1)$ space; no procedure has a smaller worst-case bar.

\paragraph{D. Adversarial verification.} Evaluate a candidate estimator on the colliding ladder and record $\max(|E(x)-L|,|E(x)-(L+\eta)|)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:minimax} this is at least $\eta/2$, so any implementation reporting less contains a bug.

\section{Discussion}

\textbf{What went wrong with the conjecture.} It silently imported the exact-amplitude intuition: under saturation an alternating sum of $m$ terms of size exactly $\eta$ telescopes and the mean decays like $1/m$; under mere boundedness the same sum can be $\eta\lceil m/2\rceil$, because the adversary sets the wrong-signed terms to zero. Sign structure constrains the direction of the error, not its magnitude, and cancellation needs magnitude.

\textbf{Where block balancing does succeed.} The majority-count law gives the plain mean an error $\eta\max(A,B)/(K+1)$ approaching $\eta$ for lopsided patterns. Block balancing caps the error at $\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$, removes block lengths from the bound, and is uniformly within a factor two of $\eta/2$. It cannot buy consistency, because consistency is not for sale.

\textbf{Why the midrange wins.} Bounded noise is a set-membership rather than distributional uncertainty model, and there the optimal estimator is the Chebyshev centre of the feasible set. Here the feasible set of floors is essentially $[\max_k\rho_k-\eta,\ \min_k\rho_k+\eta]$, whose centre is the midrange and whose half-width is at most $\eta/2$.

\textbf{Practical reading.} (i) A rebound of size $\delta$ proves $\eta\ge\delta$ and caps floor resolution at $\pm\delta/2$. (ii) More rungs of the same ladder do not sharpen the floor; only reducing $\eta$, or arranging saturated residuals, does. (iii) If a single number with a defensible bar is required, quote the midrange with $\pm\eta/2$.

\textbf{Scope.} All results are worst-case over the admissible class. Under independent zero-mean residuals, averaging recovers its usual $1/\sqrt K$ behaviour, because independence forbids the coordinated silencing exploited here. The theorems delimit what may be claimed \emph{without} such a hypothesis.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Joint identifiability.} Describe the joint feasible set of pairs $(L,\lambda)$ compatible with a finite ladder at noise $\eta$, with its Chebyshev centre and coordinate diameters.
  \item \textbf{A lower-amplitude hypothesis.} Assume $\eta'\le|s_k|\le\eta$. The ladder $P$ becomes inadmissible and the worst case should interpolate between $\eta/2$ and $\eta/m$; find the exact interpolant.
  \item \textbf{Optimal windowing.} In a genuine fade, early rungs violate $|s_k|\le\eta$; choose a trailing window long enough to contain both signs and short enough to exclude the transient, and certify the resulting adaptive bar.
  \item \textbf{Multi-dial pooling.} Several dials sharing a floor plausibly permit genuine decay in the number of dials, since collisions cannot be aligned across independent dials.
  \item \textbf{Randomised procedures.} Extend the two-point collision argument to randomised estimators, in expectation over their own randomness.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A single positive step refutes the slide-to-zero story and pins a noise level. Once a floor model is accepted, the block-balanced estimator obeys the sharp law $|\widehat L_{\mathrm{bb}}-L|\le\eta\lceil m/2\rceil/m$ --- block lengths irrelevant, bound attained --- which never decays below $\eta/2$ and from five blocks on exceeds the conjectured $2\eta/m$. No nonnegative weighting improves on $\eta/2$, and no procedure of any kind does, because two floors $\eta$ apart generate the same admissible ladder. The midrange attains $\eta/2$ exactly, settling the minimax rate. Decay returns only under exact amplitude, where the same estimator achieves $\eta/m$. For the recorded dial, with a measured rebound of $+0.0226$, the floor cannot be resolved better than $\pm 0.0113$ by any number of rungs.

